1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
You slept eight hours last night.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:06,680
So why do you feel like you got hit by a
truck?

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,840
The answer isn't hours — it's
architecture.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:15,280
Your brain sleeps in ninety-minute cycles:
light sleep, deep sleep, then dreaming.

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:19,320
A full night is four to six clean cycles.

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:25,600
But if your alarm drags you out of deep
sleep mid-cycle, you wake up groggy no matter

7
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:31,680
how long you slept. Alcohol, late
caffeine, and a restless room shatter those cycles

8
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:38,840
into fragments — eight hours of broken
sleep is not eight hours of sleep.

9
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:44,720
The fix researchers keep coming back to:
wake up at the same time every day, keep the

10
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,120
room dark, cool, and quiet — and let the
cycles finish.

11
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,120
Sleep isn't a bank. It's a rhythm.
